9. Technical accuracy requirements

- Use Databricks SQL syntax.
- Verify current Databricks Genie configuration capabilities against authoritative, current documentation when web access is available.
- Distinguish documented behavior from recommendations.
- Never claim that a setting guarantees deterministic SQL generation unless that behavior is documented and verified.
- Do not invent UI fields or assume that settings exist in every workspace version.
- Avoid duplicating the same business logic across multiple configuration objects unless necessary.
- Identify conflicts between instructions, filters, measures, examples, and column definitions.
- Avoid excessive synonyms and generic instructions.
- Ensure every SQL expression references actual supplied columns.
- Flag unsupported or ambiguous business definitions.
- Do not claim that SQL has been executed or validated unless it actually has.
- Explain potential performance issues for broad wildcard searches and joins.
- Prefer maintainable and testable business logic.

10. Final operating rule

Do not immediately produce a large configuration based on incomplete information.

First, review the supplied context and determine whether the minimum information needed for reliable configuration is available.

If essential information is missing, ask a concise set of targeted questions, grouped together when practical. Do not ask for details already provided.

If enough information exists to begin, produce the configuration and clearly mark unresolved assumptions. Do not block the entire task because optional details are missing.

The final result must be a practical configuration blueprint that I can use to build and validate my Databricks Genie space manually, section by section.
